issue.body:
### What happened?

When using a single `Dockerfile` with multiple named stages, `buildAndPushImage` on `awsx.ecr.Repository` does not work correctly if creating images for more than one of these stages from within the same stack (and parent component?).

Only the first image (=first referenced stage) is created and the others are just pointing to the first one.

This seems to be relevant in other cases where we want to pass custom build arguments too -- haven't tested this, but passing custom `buildOptions` for the same `Dockerfile` is most likely going to be wrong as well.

### Steps to reproduce

- `myRegistry.buildAndPublishImage({ context: '.', dockerfile: 'my-only.dockerfile', target: 'target-1' });` builds image as expected and returns a reference to it
- `myRegistry.buildAndPublishImage({ context: '.', dockerfile: 'my-only.dockerfile', target: 'target-2' });` results in the same image reference as the earlier, so pointing to a `target-1` image, not `target-2`

### Expected Behavior

- `myRegistry.buildAndPublishImage({ context: '.', dockerfile: 'my-only.dockerfile', target: 'target-1' });` builds image with `target-1` stage and returns a correct image reference to it
- `myRegistry.buildAndPublishImage({ context: '.', dockerfile: 'my-only.dockerfile', target: 'target-2' });` builds image with `target-2` stage and returns a correct image reference to it

### Actual Behavior

This is related to the way the internal image references are derived -- using a SHA1 of Docker command arguments where `target` property is not included in the hash input: https://github.com/pulumi/pulumi-awsx/blob/601f13634a0f7bf6472594ebdeea3260b71decc5/awsx/ecr/image.ts#L98

I thought I can perhaps manipulate the `imageName` directly, but `DockerBuild` structures from `pulumi-awsx` and `pulumi-docker` are confusing and incompatible and it turned out that it cannot be customized. So I tried to manipulate `extraOptions` but that does not seem to be included with `args` (internal) that is also used to calculate the hash. The last resort was to simply symlink my original Dockerfile with some other name which worked eventually...

### Versions used

My env details are listed below, but `master` seems to be the same...

```console
CLI          
Version      3.18.1
Go Version   go1.17.3
Go Compiler  gc

Plugins
NAME        VERSION
aws         4.5.1
docker      3.0.0
eks         0.30.0
kubernetes  3.2.0
nodejs      unknown

[...]

NAME            VERSION
@pulumi/pulumi  3.3.1
@types/node     10.17.60
chai            4.3.4
mocha           8.4.0
ts-node         10.0.0
@pulumi/awsx    0.30.0
@pulumi/eks     0.30.0
js-yaml         4.1.0
@types/chai     4.2.18
@types/mocha    8.2.2
```

### Additional context

I believe the right thing to do is to fix [getImageName](https://github.com/pulumi/pulumi-awsx/blob/601f13634a0f7bf6472594ebdeea3260b71decc5/awsx/ecr/image.ts#L98) so that it also reflects `target` option as well as `extraOptions`.

Perhaps something like this?
```typescript
function getImageName(pathOrBuild: string | pulumi.Unwrap<docker.DockerBuild>) {
    // Produce a hash of the build context and use that for the image name.
    let buildSig: string;
    if (typeof pathOrBuild === "string") {
        buildSig = pathOrBuild;
    }
    else {
        buildSig = pathOrBuild.context || ".";
        if (pathOrBuild.dockerfile) {
            buildSig += `;dockerfile=${pathOrBuild.dockerfile}`;
        }
        if (pathOrBuild.target) {
            buildSig += `;target=${pathOrBuild.target}`;
        }
        if (pathOrBuild.args) {
            for (const arg of Object.keys(pathOrBuild.args)) {
                buildSig += `;arg[${arg}]=${pathOrBuild.args[arg]}`;
            }
        }
        if (pathOrBuild.extraOptions) {  // Why is this called extraOptions and not extraArgs anyway?
            // NOTE: some args (like -t?) can be specified multiple times, but that should not be a problem as long as it is ordered the same way
            for (const xarg of Object.keys(pathOrBuild.extraOptions)) {
                buildSig += `;xarg[${xarg}]=${pathOrBuild.extraOptions[xarg]}`;
            }
        }
    }

    buildSig += `;${pulumi.getStack()}`;
    return `${utils.sha1hash(buildSig)}-container`;
}
```

### Contributing

Vote on this issue by adding a 👍 reaction. 
To contribute a fix for this issue, leave a comment (and link to your pull request, if you've opened one already).

